(function(){
  const L=(id,category,title,level,description,objectives,sections,code,reference,mistakes,task,q,options,answer,explanation)=>({id,category,title,level,summary:description.split('.')[0]+'.',keywords:[title,...objectives,...reference.flat()].join(' '),description,objectives,points:objectives,sections:sections.map(([title,body])=>({title,body})),code,reference,mistakes,task,minutes:Math.max(15,sections.length*6),quiz:{question:q,options,answer,explanation}});
  window.APEX_CURRICULUM=window.APEX_CURRICULUM||{};
  window.APEX_CURRICULUM.dbms=[
    L('dbms-foundations','Foundations','DBMS purpose, components, and users','Beginner','Understand why database management systems exist and how their major parts cooperate.',
      ['Distinguish a database from a DBMS','Explain controlled persistence, sharing, integrity, security, and recovery','Identify the query processor, storage manager, catalog, and transaction manager','Recognize database user and administrator roles'],[
        ['Database versus DBMS','A database is an organized body of related data. A DBMS is the software layer that defines, stores, queries, protects, coordinates, and recovers that data. Compared with unrelated files, a DBMS centralizes rules, reduces uncontrolled duplication, supports concurrent users, and provides declarative access.'],
        ['Core components','The parser and optimizer translate a query into an execution plan. The storage manager works with files, pages, records, indexes, and buffers. The transaction manager coordinates concurrent work, while logging and recovery preserve committed changes after failures. The system catalog stores metadata about schemas, constraints, statistics, and privileges.'],
        ['People and workloads','Designers model data, developers build applications, analysts query it, end users perform business tasks, and DBAs manage access, performance, capacity, backup, and recovery. OLTP systems favor short reliable transactions; analytical systems scan and aggregate large histories.']],
      "Application -> SQL/API -> query processor -> storage manager -> database files\n                         |-> catalog\n                         |-> transaction and recovery managers",
      [['database','Organized collection of related data'],['DBMS','Software that manages database definition and use'],['catalog','Metadata about database objects'],['query processor','Parses, optimizes, and executes requests'],['storage manager','Controls physical data access'],['DBA','Database administrator']],
      ['Calling a spreadsheet or file folder a full DBMS','Assuming the DBMS removes the need for data design','Treating backup and access control as optional application features'],
      'Map the users, data, rules, transactions, reports, and recovery needs of a small library system.',
      'Where does a DBMS normally store table and index metadata?',['Application source','System catalog','Transaction result set','User interface'],1,'The system catalog is the DBMS-managed repository for schema and operational metadata.'),

    L('architecture','Foundations','Three-schema architecture and data independence','Beginner','Learn how external views, the conceptual schema, and physical storage separate user needs from implementation details.',
      ['Describe external, conceptual, and internal schema levels','Distinguish logical from physical data independence','Explain mappings and views','Recognize centralized and client-server deployment shapes'],[
        ['Three levels','External schemas present role-specific views. The conceptual schema describes the organization-wide logical structure, relationships, and constraints. The internal schema describes files, pages, indexes, partitions, compression, and placement. Mappings translate operations across these levels.'],
        ['Data independence','Physical data independence means storage changes such as adding an index need not change tables or applications. Logical data independence means conceptual changes can be absorbed by compatible external views or mappings; it is harder because applications depend on names and structures.'],
        ['Deployment architecture','A two-tier client may connect directly to the database. Three-tier systems place application services between clients and the DBMS to centralize authorization, validation, pooling, and business logic. Distribution, replication, and cloud services change placement but not the need for clear logical boundaries.']],
      "External views: student portal | instructor portal | finance report\n                         ↓ mappings\nConceptual schema: Student, Course, Enrollment\n                         ↓ mappings\nInternal schema: pages, indexes, partitions",
      [['external schema','User or role-specific representation'],['conceptual schema','Global logical design'],['internal schema','Physical representation'],['physical independence','Change storage without changing logical schema'],['logical independence','Change logical schema without breaking views'],['mapping','Translation between schema levels']],
      ['Confusing a UI screen with the whole conceptual schema','Assuming every schema change is logically independent','Letting clients connect with unrestricted database credentials'],
      'Draw the three schema levels for a hospital and list one change that belongs at each level.',
      'Adding an index without changing application queries demonstrates what?',['Logical data independence','Physical data independence','Referential integrity','Entity specialization'],1,'An index changes the internal representation while the logical schema stays the same.'),

    L('data-models','Modeling','Data models and the relational model','Beginner','Compare major data-model families and master the basic vocabulary of relational databases.',
      ['Define relation, tuple, attribute, domain, degree, and cardinality','State relational model properties','Compare relational, document, key-value, graph, and wide-column models','Separate a schema from its current state'],[
        ['Relational vocabulary','A relation is represented as a table; a tuple is a row; an attribute is a named column; and a domain is the permitted set and meaning of values. Degree is the number of attributes, while cardinality is the number of tuples. A schema defines structure; an instance or state is the data at a moment.'],
        ['Relational principles','Rows are conceptually unordered, columns are identified by name, each cell contains one value from its domain, and duplicate tuples are not part of the mathematical relation. SQL tables can diverge through duplicate rows and null markers, so constraints and keys are essential.'],
        ['Choosing a model','Relational systems excel at integrity, joins, and flexible declarative queries. Document stores keep nested aggregates, key-value stores optimize direct lookup, graph databases prioritize traversals, and wide-column systems scale sparse partitioned records. Workload, consistency, relationships, and access patterns should drive the choice.']],
      "STUDENT(StudentId, Name, Email)\nCOURSE(CourseId, Title, Credits)\nENROLLMENT(StudentId, CourseId, EnrolledOn, Grade)",
      [['relation','Set of tuples sharing attributes'],['tuple','One row'],['attribute','Named column'],['domain','Allowed values and meaning'],['degree','Number of attributes'],['cardinality','Number of tuples'],['schema','Structure and rules'],['instance','Current data']],
      ['Using cardinality to mean number of columns','Choosing NoSQL only because data is large','Treating a current data sample as the schema'],
      'Represent the same learning platform as a relational model and a document model, then compare their strengths.',
      'What is the degree of a relation?',['Number of rows','Number of attributes','Number of keys','Number of joins'],1,'Degree counts attributes; cardinality counts tuples.'),

    L('er-basics','Modeling','ER modeling: entities, attributes, and relationships','Beginner','Turn real-world requirements into a clear entity-relationship model before creating tables.',
      ['Identify entity types and entity instances','Classify simple, composite, multivalued, derived, and key attributes','Name relationships with meaningful verbs','Avoid storing the same fact in several places'],[
        ['Entities and attributes','An entity is a distinguishable thing such as Student or Course; an entity type describes the set. Attributes record facts. Composite attributes have parts, multivalued attributes may have several values, derived attributes can be calculated, and key attributes distinguish instances. Model stable business concepts, not every screen field.'],
        ['Relationships','A relationship connects entity types and should express a business fact: Student enrolls in Course. A relationship may have attributes such as EnrolledOn or Grade. When a many-to-many relationship carries its own facts, it commonly becomes an associative entity during relational mapping.'],
        ['Requirements first','Interview stakeholders for definitions, identifiers, optionality, history, volume, and rules. Resolve synonyms and homonyms in a data dictionary. Validate the model with sample scenarios, including exceptions, before choosing column types or indexes.']],
      "Student --< Enrollment >-- Course\nStudent: StudentId, Name, Email\nCourse: CourseId, Title\nEnrollment: EnrolledOn, Status, Grade",
      [['entity type','Class of distinguishable objects'],['attribute','Property of an entity or relationship'],['composite attribute','Attribute with meaningful subparts'],['multivalued attribute','May hold several values'],['derived attribute','Calculated from other facts'],['relationship','Association among entities']],
      ['Making every noun an entity','Duplicating a calculable value without a maintenance rule','Modeling a many-to-many relationship as one repeating column'],
      'Interview an imaginary course administrator and produce an ER diagram plus a ten-term data dictionary.',
      'Where should Grade belong if it describes a student taking a particular course?',['Student','Course','Enrollment relationship','Department'],2,'Grade depends on a particular student-course enrollment, not either entity alone.'),

    L('er-advanced','Modeling','Cardinality, participation, weak entities, and specialization','Intermediate','Express advanced business rules precisely in an ER model.',
      ['Read one-to-one, one-to-many, and many-to-many cardinalities','Distinguish optional from mandatory participation','Model weak entities and identifying relationships','Use specialization, generalization, and disjointness constraints'],[
        ['Cardinality and participation','Minimum and maximum participation describe how many related instances are allowed: 0..1, 1..1, 0..*, or 1..*. Cardinality is not guessed from current data; it is a rule. Total participation means every entity instance must participate, while partial participation allows absence.'],
        ['Weak entities','A weak entity cannot be uniquely identified by its partial key alone and depends on an owner. OrderLine may use LineNumber only within an Order, giving the full identifier (OrderId, LineNumber). The identifying relationship and existence dependency should be explicit.'],
        ['Specialization','A supertype holds shared attributes and subtypes add specific ones. Constraints say whether subtypes are disjoint or overlapping and whether every supertype member must belong to a subtype. Prefer specialization only where distinct rules or attributes justify it.']],
      "Employee\n  ├─ Instructor(TeachingArea)\n  └─ Administrator(AccessLevel)\nConstraint: disjoint, partial\n\nOrder 1 ---- 1..* OrderLine (weak; partial key LineNo)",
      [['1:1','At most one on both sides'],['1:N','One parent to many children'],['M:N','Many on both sides'],['total participation','Every instance must participate'],['weak entity','Identity depends on owner'],['specialization','Supertype divided into subtypes']],
      ['Reading current row counts as cardinality rules','Using nullable subtype columns instead of justified subtypes without analysis','Giving a weak entity an incomplete identifier'],
      'Extend a university ER model with departments, employees, dependent records, and instructor/administrator subtypes.',
      'What identifies an OrderLine with partial key LineNo?',['LineNo alone','OrderId alone','OrderId plus LineNo','ProductId plus quantity'],2,'A weak entity combines its owner key with its partial key.'),

    L('schema-mapping','Modeling','Mapping ER models to relational schemas','Intermediate','Convert conceptual models into relations without losing identifiers, cardinalities, or optionality.',
      ['Map regular and weak entities','Map 1:1, 1:N, and M:N relationships','Choose strategies for multivalued attributes and subtypes','Verify constraints after mapping'],[
        ['Entity mapping','A regular entity becomes a table and its identifier becomes a primary key. Composite attributes are expanded into components. Multivalued attributes normally become a separate table containing the owner key and one value per row. Derived attributes are usually calculated unless persistence is justified.'],
        ['Relationship mapping','For 1:N, place the foreign key on the many side and include relationship attributes there. For M:N, create an associative table with foreign keys to both sides and its own attributes. For 1:1, choose the side that minimizes nulls and enforces required participation, then add uniqueness.'],
        ['Subtype mapping','Options include one table for the hierarchy with a type discriminator, one supertype plus subtype tables sharing the key, or concrete subtype tables. Choose based on completeness, disjointness, query shape, constraints, and evolution—not habit.']],
      "CREATE TABLE Enrollment (\n  StudentId bigint NOT NULL,\n  CourseId bigint NOT NULL,\n  EnrolledOn date NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (StudentId, CourseId),\n  FOREIGN KEY (StudentId) REFERENCES Student(StudentId),\n  FOREIGN KEY (CourseId) REFERENCES Course(CourseId)\n);",
      [['1:N mapping','Foreign key on N side'],['M:N mapping','Associative relation'],['1:1 mapping','Foreign key plus UNIQUE'],['multivalued attribute','Separate owner-value relation'],['subtype table','Shares supertype key'],['discriminator','Column indicating subtype']],
      ['Adding comma-separated multivalues to one column','Forgetting UNIQUE when mapping a 1:1 relationship','Mapping a relationship but dropping its attributes'],
      'Map a complete ER diagram to SQL tables and annotate how every cardinality and participation rule is enforced.',
      'How is an M:N relationship normally mapped?',['One nullable column','An associative table with both foreign keys','Two views only','A repeated JSON string'],1,'An associative relation represents each valid pair and any relationship attributes.'),

    L('keys','Relational Design','Keys: every important type and design tradeoff','Beginner','Master the complete family of relational keys and choose identifiers that remain correct as data evolves.',
      ['Distinguish super, candidate, primary, alternate, foreign, unique, simple, and composite keys','Compare natural, business, surrogate, and synthetic identifiers','Explain nullability, uniqueness, minimality, stability, and scope','Design referenced keys and cascading actions safely'],[
        ['Uniqueness hierarchy','A superkey is any attribute set that uniquely identifies a tuple, even with unnecessary attributes. A candidate key is a minimal superkey: removing any attribute breaks uniqueness. One candidate key is selected as the primary key; the remaining candidate keys are alternate keys and should still receive UNIQUE constraints. A simple key has one attribute; a composite or compound key has several.'],
        ['Meaning and origin','A natural or business key comes from the domain, such as an officially stable code. A surrogate or synthetic key is created by the system, such as an identity integer or UUID. Surrogates simplify references but do not remove business uniqueness rules: a PersonId does not prevent two rows with the same required national identifier unless that identifier is also constrained. A secondary key is a non-unique search attribute, not an identifier.'],
        ['Foreign and referenced keys','A foreign key contains values that must match a candidate key in another or the same relation, unless permitted null. It can be simple, composite, self-referencing, or shared as part of an identifying relationship. The referenced columns must be unique as a set, their types and meaning must align, and ON DELETE or ON UPDATE behavior must reflect business ownership.'],
        ['Choosing well','A good primary key is unique, never null, stable, compact, and available for every row. Natural keys can communicate meaning but may be long, editable, sensitive, or governed externally. Surrogate keys provide stability but can hide duplicates. Composite keys are often ideal for pure junction tables; a surrogate may be added when other tables must reference an individual association.']],
      "CREATE TABLE Student (\n  StudentId bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY, -- surrogate, simple\n  StudentNumber varchar(20) NOT NULL UNIQUE,                -- candidate/alternate natural key\n  Email varchar(254) NOT NULL UNIQUE                         -- another candidate key\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE Enrollment (\n  StudentId bigint NOT NULL,\n  CourseId bigint NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (StudentId, CourseId),                         -- composite candidate key\n  FOREIGN KEY (StudentId) REFERENCES Student(StudentId)\n);",
      [['superkey','Any attribute set that uniquely identifies a row'],['candidate key','Minimal superkey'],['primary key','Chosen candidate key; unique and not null'],['alternate key','Candidate key not chosen as primary'],['foreign key','References a candidate key'],['simple key','One attribute'],['composite key','Several attributes'],['natural/business key','Identifier meaningful in the domain'],['surrogate/synthetic key','System-created identifier'],['unique key','SQL uniqueness constraint; null behavior is DB-specific'],['secondary key','Non-unique attribute used for retrieval'],['partial key','Distinguishes a weak entity within its owner']],
      ['Adding a surrogate key but omitting the real business UNIQUE constraint','Calling every indexed column a key','Using mutable email or phone as a primary key without considering change','Referencing only part of a composite candidate key','Assuming UNIQUE treats nulls identically in every DBMS'],
      'Design keys for students, courses, enrollments, editions, and order lines. List every candidate key and justify the primary-key choice.',
      'If {Email} and {StudentNumber} each uniquely identify a student and StudentNumber is primary, what is Email?',['Foreign key','Alternate candidate key','Partial key','Secondary key only'],1,'Email remains a candidate key and becomes an alternate key when another candidate is selected as primary.'),

    L('constraints','Relational Design','Domains, nulls, and integrity constraints','Beginner','Use database constraints as executable business rules instead of relying only on application code.',
      ['Apply domain, key, entity, and referential integrity','Use NOT NULL, CHECK, UNIQUE, PRIMARY KEY, and FOREIGN KEY','Reason about SQL null and three-valued logic','Choose RESTRICT, CASCADE, SET NULL, or SET DEFAULT deliberately'],[
        ['Constraint families','Domain constraints limit type and permitted values. Key constraints ensure uniqueness, entity integrity prevents a primary key from being null, and referential integrity prevents orphan references. CHECK expresses row-level predicates; assertions across many rows may need triggers or carefully designed transactions where unsupported.'],
        ['Null and unknown','Null represents missing or inapplicable information, not zero or an empty string. Comparisons with null yield UNKNOWN, so use IS NULL. In a WHERE clause, only TRUE survives; FALSE and UNKNOWN are filtered. Excessive nullable columns often signal mixed entity types or unclear requirements.'],
        ['Referential actions','RESTRICT or NO ACTION blocks a parent change when children exist, CASCADE propagates deletion or key change, SET NULL removes the reference, and SET DEFAULT assigns a valid default. Cascades are powerful ownership rules and should be traced across the entire dependency graph.']],
      "CREATE TABLE Course (\n  CourseId bigint PRIMARY KEY,\n  Code varchar(20) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n  Credits smallint NOT NULL CHECK (Credits BETWEEN 1 AND 12),\n  Status varchar(12) NOT NULL CHECK (Status IN ('draft','active','retired'))\n);",
      [['NOT NULL','Requires a value'],['CHECK','Requires a predicate not to be false'],['UNIQUE','Prevents duplicate key values'],['PRIMARY KEY','Unique row identity'],['FOREIGN KEY','Requires a matching referenced key'],['UNKNOWN','Third SQL truth value']],
      ['Using a default to hide genuinely missing required data','Duplicating constraints only in one application','Applying cascading deletes without analyzing the dependency chain'],
      'Create database constraints for a booking system, including date order, status values, uniqueness, and ownership behavior.',
      'How do you test for a null value in SQL?',['= NULL','IS NULL','== NULL','NULL()'],1,'SQL uses IS NULL because ordinary comparison with null is UNKNOWN.'),

    L('functional-dependencies','Relational Design','Functional dependencies, closures, and minimal covers','Intermediate','Use functional dependencies to reason formally about keys and redundancy.',
      ['Interpret X determines Y','Compute attribute closure','Find candidate keys','Derive a minimal or canonical cover'],[
        ['Dependencies','A functional dependency X → Y means any two tuples agreeing on X must agree on Y. It is a semantic rule over all valid states, not a pattern noticed in a sample. Trivial dependencies have Y contained in X; nontrivial ones reveal useful determination.'],
        ['Armstrong reasoning and closure','Reflexivity, augmentation, and transitivity are sound and complete inference rules. To compute X+, start with X and repeatedly add attributes whose dependency determinants are already present. If X+ contains every relation attribute, X is a superkey; test minimality to find candidate keys.'],
        ['Minimal cover','Split dependencies so each right side has one attribute, remove extraneous determinant attributes, and remove redundant dependencies. A minimal cover preserves the closure of the original set and supports normalization synthesis, though equivalent minimal covers may differ.']],
      "Relation R(StudentId, CourseId, InstructorId, InstructorOffice, Grade)\nFDs:\n(StudentId, CourseId) -> Grade\nCourseId -> InstructorId\nInstructorId -> InstructorOffice\n\n(StudentId, CourseId)+ contains every attribute, so it is a candidate key.",
      [['X → Y','X functionally determines Y'],['determinant','Left side of an FD'],['closure X+','Attributes implied by X'],['extraneous attribute','Can be removed without changing implication'],['minimal cover','Equivalent irreducible FD set'],['candidate key','Minimal determinant of all attributes']],
      ['Inferring dependencies from a tiny sample','Stopping after proving a superkey without testing minimality','Removing an FD without checking whether the remaining set still implies it'],
      'For a supplied relation and FD set, compute three closures, identify every candidate key, and produce a minimal cover.',
      'When is X a superkey of relation R?',['X contains the primary-key name','X+ contains every attribute of R','X appears in every row','X has one attribute'],1,'Closure shows all attributes functionally determined by X.'),

    L('normalization-123','Relational Design','Normalization: 1NF, 2NF, and 3NF','Intermediate','Decompose relations to reduce update, insertion, and deletion anomalies while preserving meaning.',
      ['Recognize repeating groups and atomic domain violations','Remove partial dependencies for 2NF','Remove problematic transitive dependencies for 3NF','Check lossless join and dependency preservation'],[
        ['Why normalize','Redundant facts cause update anomalies, prevent inserts without unrelated data, and make deletes erase facts accidentally. Normalization uses dependencies to give each relation a focused meaning. It is a design process, not a guarantee that all queries become faster.'],
        ['First and second normal form','1NF requires one value from the column domain per row and no repeating column groups. A 1NF relation is in 2NF when every non-prime attribute depends on the whole of every candidate key, eliminating partial dependencies on part of a composite key. With only single-attribute candidate keys, 2NF is automatic.'],
        ['Third normal form','A relation is in 3NF when for every nontrivial FD X → A, X is a superkey or A is prime. Informally, non-key facts should not depend transitively on a key through another non-key fact. A good decomposition must be lossless; dependency preservation is desirable so rules can be checked without joins.']],
      "Before: Enrollment(StudentId, StudentName, CourseId, CourseTitle, InstructorId, InstructorOffice, Grade)\nAfter:\nStudent(StudentId, StudentName)\nCourse(CourseId, CourseTitle, InstructorId)\nInstructor(InstructorId, InstructorOffice)\nEnrollment(StudentId, CourseId, Grade)",
      [['1NF','Atomic values and no repeating groups'],['2NF','No partial dependency of non-prime attributes'],['3NF','Determinant is superkey or dependent is prime'],['prime attribute','Part of some candidate key'],['lossless join','Decomposition reconstructs exactly'],['dependency preservation','FDs enforceable without joining']],
      ['Splitting tables merely because they are wide','Applying 2NF rules without first finding all candidate keys','Creating a decomposition that invents spurious tuples on join'],
      'Normalize a sales-order relation from unnormalized form through 3NF, documenting FDs, keys, and lossless joins.',
      'What anomaly occurs when deleting the last enrollment also deletes the only stored course description?',['Update anomaly','Deletion anomaly','Phantom read','Deadlock'],1,'A deletion anomaly unintentionally removes an independent fact.'),

    L('normalization-advanced','Relational Design','BCNF, 4NF, 5NF, and denormalization','Advanced','Handle anomalies that remain beyond 3NF and know when controlled redundancy is justified.',
      ['Apply the BCNF determinant rule','Recognize independent multivalued dependencies','Understand join dependencies and 5NF','Denormalize only with measurement and maintenance rules'],[
        ['BCNF','A relation is in Boyce-Codd normal form when every determinant of a nontrivial FD is a superkey. BCNF is stricter than 3NF and removes certain overlapping-candidate-key anomalies, but a BCNF decomposition can sacrifice dependency preservation.'],
        ['Fourth and fifth normal forms','4NF requires every determinant of a nontrivial multivalued dependency to be a superkey; it separates independent many-valued facts such as an instructor having languages and certifications. 5NF addresses nontrivial join dependencies where information is reconstructed from three or more projections. It is uncommon but important in complex relationship designs.'],
        ['Controlled denormalization','Duplicating a derived total, snapshot, or lookup value may improve a measured workload, but define its source of truth, refresh timing, concurrency behavior, and repair process. Materialized views and summary tables can make redundancy explicit and governable.']],
      "InstructorFact(InstructorId, Language, Certification)\nIf languages and certifications vary independently, decompose to:\nInstructorLanguage(InstructorId, Language)\nInstructorCertification(InstructorId, Certification)",
      [['BCNF','Every nontrivial determinant is a superkey'],['MVD X ↠ Y','Y values independent of remaining attributes for X'],['4NF','No non-key multivalued determinants'],['join dependency','Relation constrained by joins of projections'],['5NF','No nontrivial join dependency except keys'],['denormalization','Intentional managed redundancy']],
      ['Assuming 3NF and BCNF are identical','Using 4NF without proving independent multivalued facts','Denormalizing without a consistency owner and refresh plan'],
      'Analyze a supplier-part-project example for BCNF and 5NF, then propose one measured denormalization with safeguards.',
      'What is the BCNF rule for a nontrivial FD X → Y?',['Y must be nullable','X must be a superkey','Y must be indexed','X must be foreign'],1,'BCNF requires every nontrivial determinant to identify the entire row.'),

    L('relational-algebra','Query Theory','Relational algebra and relational calculus','Intermediate','Understand the formal operations behind relational queries and optimizer rewrites.',
      ['Use selection, projection, rename, product, joins, and set operations','Explain closure of relational operations','Translate a query between algebra and SQL','Distinguish procedural algebra from declarative calculus'],[
        ['Core operators','Selection σ filters tuples, projection π chooses attributes, rename ρ changes names, union ∪ combines compatible relations, difference − subtracts, and Cartesian product × pairs every row. Joins combine selection with product; natural join matches same-named attributes but can be fragile when schemas evolve.'],
        ['Derived operators','Intersection, theta join, equijoin, outer joins, semijoin, antijoin, division, grouping, and aggregation extend practical expressiveness. Relational algebra is closed: every operator returns a relation, so expressions compose.'],
        ['Logic and optimization','Tuple and domain relational calculus state what result satisfies a predicate rather than an operation order. SQL is declarative in this spirit. Optimizers use equivalences such as pushing selective predicates toward base relations, while preserving null and duplicate semantics.']],
      "π Student.Name ( σ Course.Code='DB101' (Student ⋈ Enrollment ⋈ Course) )\n\nSELECT s.Name\nFROM Student s\nJOIN Enrollment e ON e.StudentId=s.StudentId\nJOIN Course c ON c.CourseId=e.CourseId\nWHERE c.Code='DB101';",
      [['σ selection','Filter rows'],['π projection','Choose columns'],['ρ rename','Rename relation or attributes'],['× product','All tuple pairs'],['⋈ join','Combine matching tuples'],['÷ division','Queries involving all related values'],['closure','Operator result is a relation']],
      ['Confusing selection with projection','Using natural join without reviewing every common column name','Assuming SQL bag semantics exactly match mathematical sets'],
      'Write relational-algebra expressions and SQL for five queries, including “students who completed every required course.”',
      'Which algebra operator chooses attributes?',['Selection','Projection','Difference','Product'],1,'Projection chooses attributes; selection chooses tuples.'),

    L('sql-ddl','SQL','SQL DDL, data types, and schema evolution','Beginner','Define schemas with portable, precise types and evolve them safely.',
      ['Create and alter schemas, tables, and constraints','Choose data types by meaning and range','Use defaults and generated identity deliberately','Plan backward-compatible migrations'],[
        ['DDL objects','CREATE defines schemas, tables, sequences, indexes, and views; ALTER changes them; DROP removes them. Namespaces organize ownership and avoid collisions. Put core invariants in named constraints so tools and error messages can identify them.'],
        ['Data types','Use integer ranges that fit identifiers and counts, DECIMAL precision/scale for exact values, DATE for calendar dates, timestamps with an explicit time-zone policy, bounded text where the limit has meaning, and binary types for bytes. Avoid storing dates or numbers in text merely for convenience.'],
        ['Migration strategy','Production changes must account for old and new application versions, table size, locks, validation cost, and rollback. An expand-migrate-contract sequence adds compatible structure, backfills and verifies data, switches readers/writers, then removes old structure later.']],
      "CREATE TABLE Course (\n  CourseId bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY,\n  Code varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  Title varchar(200) NOT NULL,\n  Fee decimal(10,2) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 CHECK (Fee >= 0),\n  CreatedAt timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  CONSTRAINT pk_course PRIMARY KEY (CourseId),\n  CONSTRAINT uq_course_code UNIQUE (Code)\n);",
      [['CREATE','Define an object'],['ALTER','Change an object'],['DROP','Remove an object'],['DECIMAL(p,s)','Exact decimal precision and scale'],['identity','DBMS-generated numeric value'],['migration','Controlled schema/data transition']],
      ['Using FLOAT for exact currency','Adding a required column to a huge table without a rollout plan','Relying on a misleading default instead of collecting a required fact'],
      'Design DDL and an expand-migrate-contract plan for adding verified email and course pricing to an active system.',
      'Which type normally represents exact currency?',['FLOAT','DECIMAL with suitable precision and scale','TEXT','BOOLEAN'],1,'DECIMAL stores exact base-10 values within its declared precision.'),

    L('sql-querying','SQL','SELECT, filtering, nulls, CASE, and ordering','Beginner','Build correct SQL queries and reason about logical processing order.',
      ['Use SELECT, FROM, WHERE, DISTINCT, ORDER BY, and LIMIT/FETCH','Write predicates with ranges, patterns, and membership','Handle null with three-valued logic','Create conditional results with CASE'],[
        ['Logical processing','A useful conceptual order is FROM/JOIN, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, SELECT, DISTINCT, ORDER BY, then limiting. This explains why many aliases created in SELECT are unavailable in WHERE. The optimizer may execute differently while preserving semantics.'],
        ['Predicates and null','Use comparison, BETWEEN, IN, LIKE, and explicit Boolean logic. BETWEEN is inclusive. NOT IN can surprise when the list contains null; NOT EXISTS is often safer. Parenthesize mixed AND and OR conditions for intent.'],
        ['Projection and ordering','Select only required columns, use aliases for clarity, and use CASE for conditional values or ordering. ORDER BY is the only guarantee of result order. Pagination needs a deterministic order with a unique tie-breaker.']],
      "SELECT StudentId, Name,\n       CASE WHEN Score >= 80 THEN 'advanced'\n            WHEN Score >= 50 THEN 'developing'\n            ELSE 'beginner' END AS Band\nFROM Student\nWHERE Active = TRUE AND (City = 'Delhi' OR City IS NULL)\nORDER BY Score DESC, StudentId ASC\nFETCH FIRST 20 ROWS ONLY;",
      [['WHERE','Filter rows before grouping'],['DISTINCT','Remove duplicate projected rows'],['CASE','Conditional expression'],['IS NULL','Null predicate'],['ORDER BY','Define result order'],['FETCH/LIMIT','Restrict row count; syntax varies']],
      ['Assuming table storage order is query order','Writing = NULL','Combining AND and OR without checking precedence','Using SELECT * in stable application interfaces'],
      'Create a searchable student report with optional filters, null-aware status logic, deterministic sorting, and pagination.',
      'What guarantees the order of a SQL result?',['Primary key existence','Insertion order','ORDER BY','An index scan'],2,'Only an ORDER BY clause makes result order part of the query.'),

    L('sql-modification','SQL','INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, MERGE, and safe changes','Beginner','Modify data accurately, transactionally, and with verifiable scope.',
      ['Insert single and multiple rows','Update and delete with precise predicates','Use returning/output features where available','Understand upsert and MERGE concurrency concerns'],[
        ['Writing rows','INSERT can provide literal rows or the result of a query. Name target columns explicitly. Generated keys can often be returned without a second lookup. Bulk loading may use specialized commands, but constraints, encoding, reject handling, and transaction size still matter.'],
        ['Safe updates and deletes','Preview the exact predicate with SELECT, check affected-row counts, and use a transaction for related work. An UPDATE without WHERE affects every row. Hard deletes remove data; soft-delete flags add filtering, uniqueness, privacy, and retention complexity.'],
        ['Upsert and merge','Database-specific conflict clauses atomically insert or update based on a unique constraint. MERGE can combine source and target actions but has engine-specific semantics and concurrency history. Define the matching key, duplicate-source behavior, and isolation requirements.']],
      "BEGIN;\nUPDATE Enrollment\nSET Status='completed', CompletedOn=CURRENT_DATE\nWHERE EnrollmentId=42 AND Status='active';\n-- verify affected row count and related facts\nCOMMIT;",
      [['INSERT','Create rows'],['UPDATE','Change matching rows'],['DELETE','Remove matching rows'],['MERGE/upsert','Conditional insert/update'],['RETURNING/OUTPUT','Return changed values'],['affected rows','Count of rows changed']],
      ['Running a broad UPDATE before previewing its predicate','Using maximum ID to discover a just-inserted key','Implementing check-then-insert without a unique constraint'],
      'Write a transaction that imports staged enrollments, rejects duplicates, reports changed rows, and can be retried safely.',
      'What is the strongest protection against duplicate business identifiers during concurrent inserts?',['A UI check','A UNIQUE constraint','Sorting first','A SELECT count alone'],1,'A database uniqueness constraint arbitrates concurrent writes atomically.'),

    L('joins','SQL','Joins: inner, outer, cross, self, and semi joins','Intermediate','Combine related tables without losing rows or multiplying them accidentally.',
      ['Write INNER, LEFT, RIGHT, FULL, CROSS, and self joins','Place predicates correctly with outer joins','Recognize one-to-many row multiplication','Express semi and anti joins with EXISTS'],[
        ['Join types','INNER JOIN keeps matches. LEFT JOIN keeps every left row and null-extends unmatched right columns; RIGHT and FULL apply corresponding preservation. CROSS JOIN returns every pair and is useful for deliberate combinations but dangerous accidentally. A self join relates rows in one table, such as employees to managers.'],
        ['Predicate placement','ON defines matching during a join; WHERE filters the joined result. Moving a right-side predicate from ON to WHERE can turn a left join effectively into an inner join by rejecting null-extended rows. State every intended key component in composite joins.'],
        ['Existence and multiplicity','EXISTS expresses “has at least one” without duplicating the outer row; NOT EXISTS expresses absence safely. Before aggregating, determine each relationship cardinality or independent one-to-many joins may multiply measures. Pre-aggregate where necessary.']],
      "SELECT s.StudentId, s.Name\nFROM Student s\nWHERE EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1 FROM Enrollment e\n  WHERE e.StudentId=s.StudentId AND e.Status='active'\n);\n\nSELECT c.Code, COUNT(e.StudentId)\nFROM Course c LEFT JOIN Enrollment e\n  ON e.CourseId=c.CourseId AND e.Status='active'\nGROUP BY c.Code;",
      [['INNER JOIN','Only matching rows'],['LEFT JOIN','All left rows plus matches'],['FULL JOIN','All rows from both sides'],['CROSS JOIN','Cartesian product'],['self join','Table joined to itself'],['semi join','Rows for which a match exists'],['anti join','Rows for which no match exists']],
      ['Filtering the nullable side of a left join in WHERE unintentionally','Joining on only one column of a composite relationship','Counting after two independent one-to-many joins without controlling multiplication'],
      'Write a course roster, a list including empty courses, a manager hierarchy, and an anti-join for students with no enrollment.',
      'Where should a right-table status filter go if unmatched left rows must remain?',['WHERE only','The LEFT JOIN ON condition','ORDER BY','SELECT alias'],1,'Filtering during ON matching preserves unmatched left rows.'),

    L('subqueries-ctes','SQL','Subqueries, CTEs, and recursive queries','Intermediate','Break complex SQL into correct scalar, tabular, correlated, and recursive parts.',
      ['Use scalar, row, table, and correlated subqueries','Choose EXISTS for existence tests','Organize queries with common table expressions','Traverse hierarchies using recursive CTEs'],[
        ['Subquery shapes','A scalar subquery must return at most one value; a table subquery can return many rows and columns. Correlated subqueries refer to an outer row and are logically evaluated per row, though optimizers may transform them. IN, ANY, ALL, EXISTS, and comparisons have different cardinality and null behavior.'],
        ['CTEs','WITH names a query expression for the following statement, improving structure and enabling staged aggregation. A CTE is not automatically materialized or faster; engine and version determine optimization. Use meaningful names and inspect plans instead of treating CTEs as performance barriers.'],
        ['Recursion','A recursive CTE has an anchor query and a recursive member joined by UNION ALL. It can traverse trees, graphs, sequences, and bill-of-materials structures. Include termination conditions, cycle prevention or detection, and depth safeguards.']],
      "WITH RECURSIVE tree AS (\n  SELECT CategoryId, ParentId, Name, 0 AS Depth\n  FROM Category WHERE ParentId IS NULL\n  UNION ALL\n  SELECT c.CategoryId, c.ParentId, c.Name, t.Depth+1\n  FROM Category c JOIN tree t ON c.ParentId=t.CategoryId\n  WHERE t.Depth < 20\n)\nSELECT * FROM tree ORDER BY Depth, Name;",
      [['scalar subquery','At most one value'],['correlated subquery','References outer query'],['EXISTS','True when subquery returns a row'],['CTE','Named query expression'],['anchor','Initial recursive rows'],['recursive member','Produces next-level rows']],
      ['Using = with a subquery that may return several rows','Assuming every CTE is materialized','Writing recursive SQL without termination or cycle protection'],
      'Build CTEs for course completion metrics and a recursive query for all prerequisites of a course.',
      'What are the two required logical parts of a recursive CTE?',['SELECT and ORDER BY','Anchor and recursive member','View and trigger','Index and constraint'],1,'The anchor seeds rows and the recursive member derives subsequent levels.'),

    L('aggregation-windows','SQL','Aggregation, grouping, and window functions','Intermediate','Summarize data while preserving correct groups, denominators, and row detail.',
      ['Use COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, and MAX correctly','Filter groups with HAVING','Understand null handling in aggregates','Apply window partitions, ordering, and frames'],[
        ['Groups and aggregates','GROUP BY forms one result group for each distinct grouping combination. WHERE filters input rows; HAVING filters groups. COUNT(*) counts rows, COUNT(column) counts non-null values, and SUM or AVG ignores null inputs but can return null for an empty group.'],
        ['Window functions','A window function calculates across related rows without collapsing them. PARTITION BY resets groups, ORDER BY defines sequence, and the frame determines which neighboring rows contribute. ROW_NUMBER, RANK, LAG, LEAD, and running aggregates support analytics cleanly.'],
        ['Correct analysis','Define the grain of both source and result. Watch join multiplication, integer division, missing dates, duplicate events, and biased denominators. Generate a calendar when zero-activity periods must appear, and pre-aggregate each independent fact set before joining summaries.']],
      "SELECT StudentId, CourseId, Score,\n  ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY CourseId ORDER BY Score DESC, StudentId) AS Position,\n  AVG(Score) OVER (PARTITION BY CourseId) AS CourseAverage,\n  Score - LAG(Score) OVER (PARTITION BY StudentId ORDER BY AttemptedAt) AS Change\nFROM Attempt;",
      [['COUNT(*)','Count rows'],['COUNT(col)','Count non-null values'],['HAVING','Filter groups'],['PARTITION BY','Define window groups'],['window frame','Rows contributing to a window calculation'],['ROW_NUMBER','Unique sequential position'],['RANK','Position with ties and gaps']],
      ['Selecting non-grouped detail columns without defined meaning','Expecting COUNT(column) to count nulls','Using a running-total default frame without understanding peer rows'],
      'Produce a monthly learning report with zero months, completion rates, rankings, moving averages, and previous-period change.',
      'Which clause filters completed groups?',['WHERE','HAVING','ORDER BY','PARTITION BY'],1,'HAVING evaluates after grouping and aggregation.'),

    L('sets-views','SQL','Set operations, views, and materialized views','Intermediate','Combine compatible results and create governed reusable query interfaces.',
      ['Use UNION, UNION ALL, INTERSECT, and EXCEPT','Check union compatibility','Create secure stable views','Plan materialized-view refresh and staleness'],[
        ['Set operations','UNION removes duplicates; UNION ALL preserves them and avoids duplicate elimination. INTERSECT returns common rows and EXCEPT or MINUS subtracts, with product-specific availability. Inputs must have the same number of compatible columns, and final names usually come from the first query.'],
        ['Views','A view stores a query definition and exposes it like a table. Views simplify interfaces, hide sensitive columns, centralize logic, and help compatibility, but nested views can obscure cost. Security depends on ownership and invoker/definer behavior in the DBMS.'],
        ['Materialized views','A materialized view stores query results physically. Refresh may be complete, incremental, scheduled, or on demand. Document freshness expectations, refresh concurrency, failure behavior, and indexes; consumers must understand that data may lag.']],
      "CREATE VIEW ActiveCourseRoster AS\nSELECT e.StudentId, e.CourseId, e.EnrolledOn\nFROM Enrollment e\nWHERE e.Status='active';\n\nSELECT Email FROM Student\nUNION\nSELECT Email FROM Instructor;",
      [['UNION','Combine and remove duplicates'],['UNION ALL','Combine and preserve duplicates'],['INTERSECT','Rows common to both'],['EXCEPT/MINUS','Rows in first but not second'],['view','Stored query definition'],['materialized view','Stored query result']],
      ['Using UNION when duplicate removal is unnecessary','Treating a view as guaranteed precomputed data','Refreshing a materialized view without a freshness agreement'],
      'Create an application-safe view, an audit union, and a materialized dashboard plan with an explicit freshness target.',
      'Which operation normally preserves duplicate rows?',['UNION','UNION ALL','INTERSECT','EXCEPT'],1,'UNION ALL appends inputs without distinct elimination.'),

    L('indexes','Performance','Indexes: B-trees, hashes, composites, and coverage','Intermediate','Design indexes for real predicates, joins, ordering, and constraints without over-indexing.',
      ['Explain clustered and secondary indexes','Compare B-tree and hash access','Order composite index columns deliberately','Evaluate selectivity, coverage, and write cost'],[
        ['Structures and access','A B-tree keeps sorted keys and supports equality, ranges, prefixes, and ordered scans. Hash indexes favor equality and generally not ranges. Clustered or primary organization influences row placement; secondary indexes store keys plus a row locator. Terminology and implementation vary by DBMS.'],
        ['Composite and covering indexes','A composite index is ordered by its column sequence. Predicates on leading columns are generally most usable, while later columns may help filtering or covering. Included/non-key columns can satisfy projections without enlarging the search key. Design from actual query shapes and ordering requirements.'],
        ['Costs and selectivity','Indexes accelerate reads but consume space, cache, and maintenance on inserts, updates, and deletes. Low-selectivity columns may still help in combinations, partial/filtered indexes, or bitmap indexes in analytical systems. Unique indexes can enforce keys, but every constraint should express semantics rather than merely performance.']],
      "CREATE INDEX ix_enrollment_course_status_student\n  ON Enrollment (CourseId, Status, StudentId);\n\n-- Supports equality on CourseId and Status, then StudentId lookup/order.\n-- Confirm with the real execution plan and workload.",
      [['B-tree','Ordered index for equality/range'],['hash index','Equality-oriented index'],['clustered index','Influences physical row organization'],['secondary index','Separate structure with row locator'],['composite index','Several ordered key columns'],['covering index','Contains everything a query needs'],['selectivity','Fraction/distribution of matching values']],
      ['Creating one index per column without workload evidence','Assuming (A,B) serves every query on B alone','Ignoring index maintenance and redundant-prefix indexes'],
      'Given ten high-value queries, propose the smallest useful index set and justify key order, coverage, and rejected indexes.',
      'Why does column order matter in a composite B-tree index?',['It changes table row count','It determines the sorted prefix available for search','It changes SQL types','It removes all nulls'],1,'The leading key order determines which predicate and ordering prefixes can be navigated efficiently.'),

    L('query-plans','Performance','Query processing, optimization, and EXPLAIN','Advanced','Read execution plans and improve slow queries using evidence instead of guesses.',
      ['Describe parsing, rewriting, costing, and execution','Recognize scans, seeks, joins, sorts, and aggregates','Compare estimated and actual cardinality','Use EXPLAIN safely and systematically'],[
        ['From SQL to plan','The DBMS parses and validates SQL, rewrites equivalent forms, enumerates candidate plans, estimates their cost from statistics, and executes a chosen physical plan. Cost is an internal estimate, not elapsed milliseconds. Parameter values, plan caching, and adaptive features can affect choices.'],
        ['Plan operators','Common operators include table/index scans, index seeks, nested-loop, hash, and merge joins, sorting, hashing, aggregation, spooling, and key lookup. No operator is inherently bad; suitability depends on row counts, order, memory, indexes, and repeated execution.'],
        ['Diagnosis workflow','Capture the exact slow statement and parameters, elapsed time, waits, and an actual plan where safe. Compare estimated versus actual rows to find cardinality errors, review predicates and conversions, verify statistics, and change one thing at a time. EXPLAIN ANALYZE executes the query in many products, so treat modifying statements carefully.']],
      "EXPLAIN ANALYZE\nSELECT e.StudentId\nFROM Enrollment e\nWHERE e.CourseId = 42 AND e.Status = 'active';\n\n-- Inspect access path, estimated vs actual rows, loops, I/O, sort/hash spills, and total time.",
      [['optimizer','Chooses a physical plan'],['statistics','Data distribution estimates'],['cardinality estimate','Predicted row count'],['nested loop','Repeated inner access per outer row'],['hash join','Build/probe hash structure'],['merge join','Combine ordered inputs'],['sargable','Predicate usable for index search']],
      ['Declaring every scan bad','Optimizing from an estimated plan without runtime evidence','Wrapping an indexed column in a function and hiding searchability','Running modifying EXPLAIN ANALYZE casually'],
      'Analyze a slow query plan, identify the largest estimate error and dominant work, then test two measurable remedies.',
      'What does a large estimated-versus-actual row mismatch often indicate?',['A syntax error','Cardinality-estimation trouble','A guaranteed deadlock','A missing primary key name'],1,'Bad row estimates can drive poor join, access, and memory choices.'),

    L('transactions-acid','Transactions','Transactions and ACID','Intermediate','Group related operations into reliable units with clear success and failure boundaries.',
      ['Define atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability','Use BEGIN, COMMIT, ROLLBACK, and savepoints','Keep transactions short but complete','Distinguish database consistency from business correctness'],[
        ['ACID','Atomicity makes a transaction all-or-nothing. Consistency means a correct transaction moves the database between states satisfying enforced invariants. Isolation controls interference among concurrent transactions. Durability means committed results survive failures under the system’s guarantee.'],
        ['Boundaries and errors','Start a transaction before the related reads and writes that require one decision. Commit only after all validations and writes succeed; rollback after errors. Savepoints allow partial rollback but do not replace a coherent unit. Client libraries differ in autocommit and error-state behavior.'],
        ['Practical design','Long transactions hold versions, locks, log space, and resources. Keep them focused, order access consistently, set timeouts, and design retries for transient conflicts. Do not make irreversible external actions and database commits pretend to be one atomic local transaction; use patterns such as outbox and idempotency.']],
      "BEGIN;\nUPDATE Account SET Balance=Balance-500 WHERE AccountId=1;\nUPDATE Account SET Balance=Balance+500 WHERE AccountId=2;\n-- verify both accounts and invariant\nCOMMIT;",
      [['atomicity','All or none'],['consistency','Preserve declared invariants'],['isolation','Control concurrent visibility/interference'],['durability','Committed work survives failures'],['savepoint','Partial rollback marker'],['autocommit','Each statement is implicitly committed']],
      ['Holding a transaction open while waiting for user input','Assuming ACID proves business logic is correct','Retrying a transaction that also sent a non-idempotent external request'],
      'Design a transfer transaction with constraints, concurrency behavior, failure handling, audit data, and an external-notification outbox.',
      'Which ACID property makes a multi-step transaction all-or-nothing?',['Consistency','Atomicity','Isolation','Durability'],1,'Atomicity prevents a partial transaction from becoming the final database state.'),

    L('schedules','Transactions','Schedules, serializability, and recoverability','Advanced','Analyze interleaved transactions formally and recognize safe concurrent histories.',
      ['Build precedence graphs for conflict serializability','Distinguish serial and serializable schedules','Define recoverable, cascadeless, and strict schedules','Recognize dirty write and dirty read dependencies'],[
        ['Schedules and conflicts','A schedule orders reads, writes, commits, and aborts from multiple transactions while preserving each transaction’s internal order. Operations conflict when they access the same item, belong to different transactions, and at least one writes. A serial schedule has no interleaving.'],
        ['Conflict serializability','Build a precedence graph with one node per transaction and an edge Ti → Tj when a conflicting Ti operation precedes Tj. An acyclic graph means the schedule is conflict-serializable, and a topological order gives an equivalent serial order. View serializability is broader but harder to test.'],
        ['Recovery properties','A recoverable schedule delays Tj commit until transactions whose writes Tj read have committed. Cascadeless schedules allow reads only from committed writes. Strict schedules prevent reading or overwriting an item written by an uncommitted transaction, simplifying recovery.']],
      "Schedule: r1(X), w1(X), r2(X), w2(Y), c1, c2\nT2 reads X written by T1, so T2 must not commit before T1.\nFor strictness, r2(X) must wait until T1 commits or aborts.",
      [['serial schedule','Transactions do not interleave'],['conflict','Same item, different transactions, at least one write'],['precedence graph','Conflict-order graph'],['conflict serializable','Acyclic precedence graph'],['recoverable','Readers commit after source writers'],['strict','No access to uncommitted written item']],
      ['Calling every interleaved schedule unsafe','Forgetting write-write conflicts','Checking serializability but ignoring commit and abort recoverability'],
      'For four supplied schedules, draw precedence graphs and classify serializability, recoverability, cascadelessness, and strictness.',
      'What graph property proves conflict serializability?',['Every node has an edge','The graph is acyclic','The graph is complete','All edges are bidirectional'],1,'An acyclic precedence graph admits an equivalent serial order.'),

    L('isolation','Transactions','Isolation levels, anomalies, and MVCC','Advanced','Choose isolation based on required invariants and understand what each level may permit.',
      ['Recognize dirty read, nonrepeatable read, phantom, lost update, and write skew','Compare standard isolation levels','Explain snapshots and multiversion concurrency control','Protect multi-row invariants correctly'],[
        ['Anomalies','A dirty read sees uncommitted data; a nonrepeatable read sees a row change between reads; a phantom changes the qualifying row set; lost update overwrites concurrent work; and write skew lets transactions update different rows after reading a shared condition. Names and guarantees vary by DBMS implementation.'],
        ['Levels','Read Uncommitted provides weak isolation, Read Committed prevents dirty reads, Repeatable Read strengthens stable reads, and Serializable aims for behavior equivalent to some serial order. Snapshot isolation provides transaction snapshots and write-write conflict detection but may still permit write skew.'],
        ['MVCC and invariants','MVCC keeps row versions so readers and writers can often proceed without blocking each other. Old versions require cleanup and long transactions can delay it. Protect invariants with serializable execution, explicit locking, atomic conditional statements, or constraint designs—not assumptions based on isolated reads.']],
      "-- Atomic seat claim avoids read-then-write race\nUPDATE Seat\nSET ClaimedBy = :student\nWHERE SeatId = :seat AND ClaimedBy IS NULL;\n-- success only when affected row count = 1",
      [['dirty read','Read uncommitted write'],['nonrepeatable read','Same row returns changed value'],['phantom','Matching row set changes'],['lost update','One write overwrites another'],['write skew','Different writes violate shared condition'],['MVCC','Maintain multiple row versions'],['snapshot','Consistent version view']],
      ['Assuming Repeatable Read means identical guarantees in every product','Using snapshot isolation for a cross-row invariant without testing write skew','Ignoring affected-row count in atomic conditional updates'],
      'Model two concurrent booking transactions under each isolation level and select a safe implementation for the seat invariant.',
      'Which anomaly can occur under snapshot isolation when transactions update different rows after reading one shared rule?',['Dirty read','Write skew','Syntax error','Torn page'],1,'Snapshot isolation can permit write skew unless the invariant creates a direct write conflict or stronger protection is used.'),

    L('locking','Transactions','Locking, deadlocks, and optimistic concurrency','Advanced','Coordinate concurrent access and recover cleanly from unavoidable conflicts.',
      ['Distinguish shared, exclusive, intent, row, page, table, and predicate locks','Explain two-phase locking','Detect, prevent, and retry deadlocks','Use optimistic version checks'],[
        ['Locking protocol','Shared locks permit compatible readers; exclusive locks protect writes. Intent locks summarize lower-level locks for hierarchy compatibility. Predicate or key-range locks protect searched ranges. Two-phase locking grows the lock set then releases it; strict 2PL keeps write locks to transaction end.'],
        ['Deadlocks','A deadlock is a cycle of transactions waiting for one another. The DBMS detects or times out and aborts a victim. Reduce frequency by using consistent access order, useful indexes, small transactions, and prompt commits. Applications must recognize the error and retry the whole transaction with bounded backoff.'],
        ['Optimistic control','Optimistic concurrency reads a version or timestamp and updates only when it is unchanged. An affected-row count of zero means conflict, not success. This works well when contention is low and users should reconcile edits; it does not replace constraints for universal invariants.']],
      "UPDATE Course\nSET Title=:title, Version=Version+1\nWHERE CourseId=:id AND Version=:expected_version;\n-- 1 row: success; 0 rows: reload and resolve conflict",
      [['shared lock','Read-compatible lock'],['exclusive lock','Write lock'],['intent lock','Signals lower-level locks'],['two-phase locking','Acquire phase then release phase'],['deadlock','Cycle in wait-for graph'],['optimistic concurrency','Validate version at write time']],
      ['Retrying only the failed statement after a deadlock','Accessing shared resources in inconsistent order','Treating optimistic conflict as successful update'],
      'Create a deadlock example, draw its wait-for graph, and redesign access order plus a safe retry and version-check strategy.',
      'What must an application normally do after the DBMS chooses its transaction as a deadlock victim?',['Continue the next statement','Retry the whole logical transaction safely','Drop every index','Disable constraints'],1,'The aborted unit must be restarted from a known transaction boundary.'),

    L('storage','Storage','Files, pages, records, and buffer management','Advanced','Understand how logical rows become physical bytes and why I/O shapes database performance.',
      ['Describe pages, extents, records, and slotted pages','Compare heap and ordered file organizations','Explain buffer pools, pinning, dirty pages, and replacement','Connect row width and locality to I/O'],[
        ['Physical layout','Storage is transferred in pages or blocks, often grouped in extents. A slotted page keeps a slot directory so variable-length records can move inside the page while row references remain stable. Records include headers, null maps, fixed fields, variable offsets, and sometimes version information.'],
        ['File organization','A heap stores rows without key order and supports fast general insertion. Ordered or clustered organization improves locality for a chosen key but adds maintenance. Overflow, forwarding, fill factor, compression, and partitioning influence space and access patterns.'],
        ['Buffer manager','The buffer pool caches database pages in memory. A miss reads from storage; dirty pages must eventually be flushed. Pinning protects pages in use, replacement policies choose victims, and write-ahead logging constrains when dirty data can reach disk. Good locality and narrow rows increase useful cache density.']],
      "Logical query -> access method -> buffer pool -> page cache hit?\n                                         | yes: use page\n                                         | no: read block from storage\nModified page = dirty; WAL record must be durable before page flush.",
      [['page/block','Basic storage transfer unit'],['extent','Group of pages'],['record','Physical representation of a row'],['slotted page','Page with record slot directory'],['heap file','Unordered record organization'],['buffer pool','Memory cache of pages'],['dirty page','Modified page not yet flushed']],
      ['Thinking a row is read from disk in isolation from its page','Assuming larger rows have no cache consequence','Confusing a database buffer pool with a query result cache'],
      'Estimate pages needed for a table under two row designs and explain the effects on scans, lookups, and cache hit rate.',
      'What is normally the basic unit transferred between storage and a DBMS buffer?',['One column','One page/block','One database','One SQL keyword'],1,'Database storage managers perform I/O in pages or blocks.'),

    L('recovery','Storage','WAL, checkpoints, crashes, and recovery','Advanced','Explain how a DBMS restores a correct state after transaction, system, or media failure.',
      ['Distinguish transaction, system, and media failures','Explain write-ahead logging','Understand redo, undo, checkpoints, and recovery phases','Relate force/no-force and steal/no-steal policies'],[
        ['Failure classes','A transaction may abort from validation or conflict; a system crash loses volatile memory but not durable storage; media failure damages persistent data and requires backup or replicas. Recovery goals depend on the failure and the durability contract.'],
        ['Write-ahead logging','WAL records describe changes and transaction state. The log record for a data change must be durable before the changed page is flushed, and commit is acknowledged only after the required commit log is durable. This permits no-force commits and recovery of dirty-page writes.'],
        ['Restart recovery','A checkpoint bounds how far recovery must inspect; it is not necessarily a complete data flush. ARIES-style recovery performs analysis to reconstruct state, redo to repeat history as needed, and undo to roll back loser transactions, recording compensation log records. Backups remain necessary for media loss.']],
      "Rules:\n1. WAL before data: flush a change log record before its dirty page.\n2. Commit before reply: flush the transaction commit record before success.\nRestart: analyze -> redo -> undo uncommitted work.",
      [['WAL','Write-ahead log'],['redo','Reapply a logged action'],['undo','Reverse uncommitted action'],['checkpoint','Recovery progress marker'],['force','Flush changed pages at commit'],['steal','Allow uncommitted dirty page eviction'],['CLR','Compensation log record']],
      ['Calling a checkpoint a backup','Acknowledging a commit before required log durability','Assuming redo alone handles every uncommitted write'],
      'Trace a log containing two commits and one crash-interrupted transaction; identify analysis, redo, and undo actions.',
      'What must reach durable storage before a dirty data page under WAL?',['The entire backup','Its relevant log record','Every index page','The application log'],1,'Write-ahead means recovery information is durable before the data page it describes.'),

    L('security','Administration','Database security, authorization, and auditing','Intermediate','Protect confidentiality, integrity, and availability through layered database controls.',
      ['Apply authentication, authorization, least privilege, and separation of duties','Use roles, grants, views, and row/column controls','Prevent SQL injection with parameters','Plan encryption, secret handling, masking, and audit trails'],[
        ['Access control','Authentication establishes identity; authorization decides permitted actions. Grant privileges to roles, assign roles to principals, and separate application runtime, migration, reporting, backup, and administration accounts. Deny-by-default and least privilege reduce impact of mistakes and compromise.'],
        ['Injection and data exposure','Bind untrusted data as parameters; parameters cannot safely replace identifiers or SQL syntax, which must come from controlled allowlists. Limit result columns and rows, avoid secrets in logs, mask non-production data, and check authorization at each data operation.'],
        ['Encryption and audit','Encrypt transport with verified TLS and protect storage and backups with managed keys. Hash passwords using a dedicated adaptive password hash, not reversible encryption. Audit security-sensitive reads and changes with protected timestamps and identities, while balancing privacy, retention, and monitoring usefulness.']],
      "-- Runtime role receives only required interface privileges\nGRANT SELECT, INSERT ON Enrollment TO apex_runtime;\nREVOKE ALL ON StudentPrivate FROM apex_runtime;\n\n-- Application code\nSELECT StudentId FROM Student WHERE Email = :email; -- bound value",
      [['authentication','Verify identity'],['authorization','Decide allowed actions'],['least privilege','Grant only necessary access'],['role','Named privilege collection'],['parameterization','Bind data separately from SQL text'],['encryption at rest','Protect stored media'],['audit trail','Protected record of important activity']],
      ['Giving the application owner or administrator privileges','Building SQL by concatenating user input','Copying real sensitive data into development without masking','Recording secrets in diagnostic logs'],
      'Design roles and an access matrix for learners, instructors, support, analysts, migrations, and DBAs; include injection and audit controls.',
      'What is the correct way to include an untrusted email value in SQL?',['String concatenation','A bound parameter','A comment','A table alias'],1,'Parameters keep data separate from executable SQL structure.'),

    L('backup-dr','Administration','Backups, restore testing, RPO, and RTO','Intermediate','Design recoverability around business loss and downtime limits, then prove restores work.',
      ['Distinguish full, differential, incremental, snapshot, and log backup concepts','Define RPO and RTO','Plan point-in-time recovery','Test restores and operational runbooks'],[
        ['Objectives','Recovery point objective (RPO) is the maximum tolerable data-loss window. Recovery time objective (RTO) is the target time to restore service. These business requirements determine backup frequency, log retention, replication, automation, and cost. High availability does not automatically provide historical recovery.'],
        ['Backup chain','A full backup captures a base; differential backups contain changes since a base; incremental backups contain changes since a prior backup; transaction-log or archive-log backups enable point-in-time recovery. Snapshots are fast but may share failure domains. Terminology differs across products.'],
        ['Proving recovery','Encrypt backups, isolate copies, control access, monitor jobs, and follow geographic and retention requirements. Regularly restore into a clean environment, run integrity and application checks, measure elapsed time, verify required logs and keys, and update a step-by-step runbook. An untested backup is only a hope.']],
      "Business target: RPO <= 15 minutes; RTO <= 2 hours\nPlan: weekly full + daily differential + continuous log archive\nTest: quarterly isolated restore, point-in-time replay, integrity checks, application smoke test, measured RTO",
      [['RPO','Maximum acceptable data-loss interval'],['RTO','Target service restoration time'],['full backup','Complete recoverable base'],['incremental','Changes since prior backup'],['differential','Changes since base'],['point-in-time recovery','Restore to a chosen moment']],
      ['Treating replication as the only backup','Keeping every backup in one failure domain','Testing backup creation but never a complete restore','Defining RPO and RTO without business owners'],
      'Create a backup and disaster-recovery plan for the learning platform, including retention, encryption, owners, tests, RPO, and RTO.',
      'What does an RPO of 15 minutes describe?',['Maximum target downtime','Maximum tolerable data-loss window','Backup file size','Query timeout'],1,'RPO states how far back the recovered data may be relative to failure.'),

    L('programmability','Administration','Procedures, functions, triggers, and generated logic','Advanced','Use server-side programmability where it strengthens integrity without hiding an unmaintainable application.',
      ['Distinguish procedures, functions, and triggers','Choose row-level versus statement-level behavior','Prevent recursive and multi-row trigger bugs','Keep side effects, errors, and deployment observable'],[
        ['Procedures and functions','Stored procedures perform named database operations and may return result sets or output parameters. Functions return values or tables and often face restrictions on side effects. They can centralize data-intensive logic and security boundaries but increase product coupling and require source control, testing, and deployment discipline.'],
        ['Triggers','Triggers run automatically on data or schema events. They can enforce cross-row rules, maintain audit facts, or integrate legacy behavior, but are invisible to callers. Understand BEFORE/AFTER/INSTEAD OF timing, row versus statement execution, multi-row input, ordering, recursion, transaction scope, and error propagation.'],
        ['Safer design','Prefer declarative constraints for simple invariants. Avoid network calls and slow work inside database transactions. Make routines set-based, idempotent where appropriate, minimally privileged, versioned, monitored, and documented. Never assume one trigger firing means one changed row.']],
      "-- Conceptual audit trigger logic (syntax varies by DBMS)\nON UPDATE OF Enrollment\nINSERT INTO EnrollmentAudit(EnrollmentId, OldStatus, NewStatus, ChangedAt, ChangedBy)\nSELECT old.Id, old.Status, new.Status, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, CURRENT_USER\nFROM old_rows old JOIN new_rows new ON new.Id=old.Id;",
      [['procedure','Callable stored operation'],['function','Routine returning a value or table'],['trigger','Automatic event-driven routine'],['row trigger','Runs conceptually for each row'],['statement trigger','Runs once with a changed set'],['generated column','Value derived by database expression']],
      ['Using a trigger where a CHECK or foreign key is sufficient','Writing trigger logic that handles only one affected row','Calling external services inside a transaction trigger','Deploying routines outside version control'],
      'Evaluate three business rules and implement each with the simplest suitable constraint, routine, or trigger plus tests.',
      'What should normally enforce a simple same-row value rule?',['A network call','A CHECK constraint','A hidden recursive trigger','A nightly script'],1,'Declarative constraints are visible, optimizable, and reliably applied to every writer.'),

    L('distributed','Distributed Data','Replication, partitioning, sharding, and CAP','Advanced','Reason about data placement, availability, consistency, and failure in distributed databases.',
      ['Distinguish replication from partitioning','Compare synchronous and asynchronous replication','Choose partition and shard keys','Interpret CAP and consistency models carefully'],[
        ['Replication','Replication keeps copies for availability, read scaling, or recovery. Synchronous replication waits for required replicas and increases durability/consistency at latency and availability cost; asynchronous replication can lag and lose recent acknowledged work during failover depending on guarantees. Reads from replicas may be stale.'],
        ['Partitioning and sharding','Horizontal partitioning divides rows, vertical partitioning divides columns, and sharding places partitions across independent nodes. A good key balances load, supports common routing, and avoids hot spots. Cross-shard joins, uniqueness, transactions, rebalancing, and global ordering become harder.'],
        ['CAP and consistency','During a network partition, a distributed system cannot guarantee both every request completes and every read observes one linearizable value. CAP concerns partition scenarios, not a general choice of only two letters. Systems also expose eventual, causal, session, quorum, or tunable behaviors; match them to each invariant.']],
      "Users sharded by hash(UserId) -> shards A, B, C\nCourse catalog replicated read-only near users\nEnrollment write routed to owning user shard\nOpen questions: cross-shard course capacity, global uniqueness, failover lag, rebalancing",
      [['replication','Maintain data copies'],['primary/leader','Coordinates writes in a replication design'],['partition','Subset of one logical dataset'],['shard','Partition placed on an independent node'],['hot spot','Disproportionate load on one key/range'],['linearizability','Operations appear in one real-time-consistent order'],['eventual consistency','Replicas converge absent new writes']],
      ['Calling a read replica a backup without retention and restore capability','Choosing a sequential shard key that creates a hot shard','Using CAP as a slogan without naming the partition behavior and invariant'],
      'Design placement for a global learning platform and document routing, lag, failover, cross-shard rules, and rebalance procedures.',
      'What problem can a poor shard key create?',['Only syntax errors','Hot spots and uneven load','Automatic normalization','Stronger foreign keys'],1,'Shard-key distribution and access patterns determine load balance and routing efficiency.'),

    L('nosql','Distributed Data','NoSQL models and polyglot persistence','Intermediate','Choose document, key-value, wide-column, or graph storage from access patterns rather than fashion.',
      ['Compare major non-relational models','Design aggregates and partition keys','Understand denormalization and consistency tradeoffs','Avoid uncontrolled polyglot complexity'],[
        ['Model families','Key-value stores retrieve opaque values by key. Document stores query nested semi-structured documents. Wide-column stores organize sparse rows by partition and clustering keys. Graph databases store vertices and edges for relationship traversal. Many modern systems borrow features across categories.'],
        ['Design from access patterns','NoSQL design often begins with required queries, scale, latency, and partition boundaries. Embed facts read and changed together, reference independently evolving aggregates, and duplicate data only with a defined update strategy. Partition-key choice is a data-model decision.'],
        ['Polyglot persistence','Using different stores can suit search, caching, graph traversal, analytics, and transactions, but each adds skills, monitoring, backup, security, data movement, and consistency failure modes. Keep a clear system of record and synchronize through reliable events or change-data capture.']],
      "Document example:\nCourse { id, title, modules:[{id,title,order}] }\nKeep enrollments separate because they grow independently and are queried by learner.\nPartition enrollment by LearnerId if learner history is the dominant access path.",
      [['key-value','Key to opaque value'],['document','Nested semi-structured aggregate'],['wide-column','Partitioned sparse row model'],['graph','Vertices and relationships'],['aggregate','Consistency and update boundary'],['partition key','Controls placement and routing'],['polyglot persistence','Multiple storage technologies']],
      ['Moving a normalized relational schema unchanged into documents','Embedding an unbounded collection','Adding databases without ownership and consistency contracts'],
      'Model course content, progress, social connections, and search in candidate stores, then justify a minimal architecture.',
      'What should primarily drive a NoSQL partition key?',['Alphabetical beauty','Required access patterns and load distribution','The first field in a form','A random table name'],1,'The key controls routing, locality, scale, and hot-spot risk.'),

    L('analytics','Analytics','OLTP, OLAP, warehouses, and dimensional modeling','Advanced','Separate operational transactions from historical analysis and design understandable facts and dimensions.',
      ['Compare OLTP and OLAP workloads','Define fact-table grain','Design dimensions and star schemas','Use slowly changing dimensions and ETL/ELT controls'],[
        ['Operational versus analytical','OLTP handles current-state, short, selective, concurrent transactions with strong integrity. OLAP scans and aggregates large historical datasets across many dimensions. Running heavy analytics on the operational primary can compete for CPU, I/O, memory, locks, and versions.'],
        ['Dimensional model','A fact table stores measurements at one declared grain such as one lesson attempt. Dimensions describe who, what, where, and when. A star schema keeps denormalized dimensions around facts for usability; snowflaking normalizes parts but adds joins. Conformed dimensions align analysis across facts.'],
        ['History and pipelines','Slowly changing dimension Type 1 overwrites, Type 2 adds versioned rows, and Type 3 keeps limited previous values. ETL transforms before loading; ELT loads then transforms. Pipelines need lineage, tests, idempotency, late-arriving-data policy, reconciliation, and privacy controls.']],
      "FactLessonAttempt grain: one learner attempt at one lesson at one time\nMeasures: DurationSeconds, Score, CompletedFlag\nDimensions: Learner, Lesson, Course, Date, Device\nNever mix daily course totals into the same fact grain.",
      [['OLTP','Operational transaction processing'],['OLAP','Analytical processing'],['grain','Exact meaning of one fact row'],['fact table','Measurements and dimension keys'],['dimension','Descriptive analysis context'],['star schema','Facts surrounded by dimensions'],['SCD Type 2','New row version preserves history']],
      ['Designing facts before declaring the grain','Mixing multiple grains in one table','Overwriting dimension history required for past reports','Building pipelines without reconciliation to source totals'],
      'Design a progress warehouse with fact grains, dimensions, SCD choices, quality tests, and three useful metrics.',
      'What must be declared before choosing columns for a fact table?',['The font','The row grain','The backup filename','The administrator password'],1,'The grain defines exactly what one fact row represents and prevents mixed meanings.'),

    L('dba-operations','Administration','DBA operations, monitoring, and capacity planning','Advanced','Operate databases with observable health, safe maintenance, and planned growth.',
      ['Monitor availability, latency, throughput, errors, saturation, and data health','Manage statistics, bloat, vacuum, and index maintenance by product','Plan capacity and connection limits','Use runbooks and change controls'],[
        ['Operational signals','Monitor user-visible success and latency plus query rate, waits, blocking, deadlocks, CPU, memory, I/O latency, storage growth, log volume, replication lag, backup status, and connection pressure. Baselines and service-level objectives make alerts actionable.'],
        ['Maintenance','Statistics must describe changing distributions for the optimizer. MVCC systems may need vacuuming or garbage collection; indexes and tables can bloat or fragment; integrity checks and patching require planned windows or online procedures. Follow the chosen DBMS rather than generic maintenance folklore.'],
        ['Capacity and change','Forecast rows, row width, indexes, logs, temp space, backups, and peak workload—not just data size. Bound connection pools, test migrations at realistic scale, stage changes, preserve rollback paths, and document incident procedures with owners and evidence collection.']],
      "Dashboard groups:\nAvailability: successful connects, failovers\nWorkload: transactions/sec, p95 latency, top queries\nContention: waits, locks, deadlocks\nResources: CPU, memory, I/O, storage, temp\nProtection: backup age, restore test, replica lag, corruption checks",
      [['SLO','Service-level objective'],['wait event','Reason work is blocked'],['replication lag','Replica delay behind source'],['statistics','Optimizer distribution metadata'],['bloat','Space retained beyond useful live data'],['connection pool','Reusable bounded sessions'],['runbook','Tested operational procedure']],
      ['Alerting on every metric without an action','Allowing unbounded application connections','Running generic rebuild jobs regardless of evidence','Testing migrations only on tiny development data'],
      'Create an operations dashboard, alert thresholds, monthly capacity forecast, and incident runbook for the platform database.',
      'Which alert is most useful?',['One with no owner','One tied to user impact and a clear response','Every metric above zero','One disabled during incidents'],1,'Actionable alerts connect symptoms to service objectives, ownership, and a response.'),

    L('design-project','Capstone','Complete database design and implementation project','Advanced','Apply requirements, modeling, normalization, SQL, performance, security, and recovery as one coherent database solution.',
      ['Move from requirements to conceptual, logical, and physical design','Implement constraints and representative transactions','Validate plans, concurrency, security, and recovery','Document decisions and tradeoffs'],[
        ['Requirements and models','Define scope, actors, use cases, terminology, data ownership, retention, volume, critical transactions, reports, and quality rules. Produce an ER model, identify keys and cardinalities, map it to relations, state functional dependencies, and normalize with documented exceptions.'],
        ['Implementation and verification','Create migrations, constraints, seed data, CRUD and analytical queries, views, roles, and the smallest useful index set. Test invalid data, concurrency conflicts, transaction rollback, query plans at realistic scale, privilege boundaries, and schema upgrades from an older version.'],
        ['Operations and communication','Set RPO/RTO, backup and restore tests, monitoring, capacity assumptions, audit requirements, and incident runbooks. Deliver a data dictionary, diagrams, decision records, sample plans, test results, and a guided demonstration so another person can operate the system.']],
      "Capstone deliverables:\n1. Requirements + data dictionary\n2. ER diagram + mapped normalized schema\n3. Versioned DDL migrations + representative data\n4. Queries, transactions, indexes, plans, and concurrency tests\n5. Roles, audit, backup/restore, monitoring, and runbook\n6. Written tradeoffs and live demonstration",
      [['conceptual design','Business entities and relationships'],['logical design','Relations, keys, dependencies, constraints'],['physical design','Types, indexes, partitions, storage choices'],['migration','Versioned change'],['verification','Evidence requirements are met'],['decision record','Context, choice, alternatives, consequences']],
      ['Starting DDL before definitions and rules are agreed','Demonstrating only happy-path inserts','Adding indexes without plan evidence','Delivering a schema with no restore or operating plan'],
      'Build the Apex learning database end to end. Include courses, topics, lessons, puzzles, attempts, progress, achievements, and search with every listed deliverable.',
      'What makes a database capstone complete?',['A diagram alone','DDL alone','A coherent design plus tested behavior, security, performance, recovery, and documentation','A large number of tables'],2,'A production-ready database is both correctly designed and demonstrably operable.' )
  ];
})();
